<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philitas of Cos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 340–c. 285 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Philetas of Cos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philitas of Cos was a Greek poet and scholar of the early Hellenistic age, born on Kos around 340 BCE. He was tutor to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> and is regarded as the first poet-scholar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, writing elegies and a glossary of rare words. Later poets such as Theocritus and Callimachus admired him, but only fragments of his work survive.</p></div>
